A||||4||Homicidal||Ummmmmmmmmmmmm.....did I burn the amp?????? ||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-13-2000||12:54 AM||||<BR>                          I was testing the power of the amp and I hooked the B+ (battery) wire to the positive and the ground to the<BR>                          negative and I don't think anything happened. So I did the same thing, but hooked the battery to the negative and<BR>                          ground to the positive. I saw a spark. Did I do something to hurt the amp? <P>                          I checked the fuse in the amp and it's fine. <BR>                          I was a dumb@ss not to realize I needed my remote turn on wire for it to show power. But now I know. <P>                          But is there a chance I fried the amp? Hope not! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||stimpy||04-13-2000||07:29 AM||||You might get lucky on this, but what where ya thinkin, buddy? (I know, you've already beat yourself up for it!) But may I make a suggestion? Put an in-line fuse holder in your power wire. This will not only protect you from some nasty electrical fires, but you can do your hookup without the fuse so you don' have to change your name to ampicidal.<BR>Anyway, take your am to the local stereo shop and they can prob bench test it if they are any good. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||DJRice||04-13-2000||08:57 AM||||Well, If the fuse aint blown, and it does not work with the amp remote and B+ hooked to positive, and the negative terminal grounded, then you probably cooked the amp.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Formula||04-13-2000||12:41 PM||||usually you can just change the mosfets and fix the amp for $2||||reg||1
Z||000004||olympic||04-13-2000||02:47 PM||||    Take it back and see if they will fix it under warranty.  You might get lucky.  I broke off a terminal on one of my amps the same day I bought it(for $750) and I took it back fully expectng to have to pay for the repair myself.  They sent it back to the manufacturer(Alpine) who fixed it for me free of charge.  Always been happy dealing with Alpine.<BR>   As to the fuses for your power wires here's a pic of my setup;<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/robtopaz04.jpg">   <P>The fuse should be as close to the battery as possible.  It only costs about $20 and is easy to install.  It's worth it because if your power wire shorts out under the dash or carpet without a fuse, your car WILL catch fire<P><p>[This message has been edited by olympic (edited 04-13-2000).]||||reg||1
